WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:13.087
Give Claude Code control of your Cloudflare account. Wire it in once, and it can read your account, change settings, deploy Workers and read live logs, all from the terminal.

2
00:00:13.087 --> 00:00:18.129
About fifteen minutes, and you do not need to find any API key to begin.

3
00:00:18.829 --> 00:00:29.847
Here is what it actually gets you. It can list and edit DNS records, create and deploy Workers, manage R2 buckets, KV namespaces and D1 databases,

4
00:00:29.847 --> 00:00:40.563
read observability logs, adjust WAF and Zero Trust, and reach almost any other part of the Cloudflare API. All by you asking in plain English.

5
00:00:41.263 --> 00:00:49.973
It does that through three things working together, and it is worth knowing which is which when something needs a nudge.

6
00:00:49.973 --> 00:00:58.538
Skills are on-demand knowledge of how Cloudflare actually works, so you are not re-explaining the platform every time.

7
00:00:58.538 --> 00:01:07.030
MCP servers are the live line into your account's API. And Wrangler is the command-line tool that builds and deploys.

8
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:11.821
One plugin install wires up the first two, and keeps them matched.

9
00:01:12.521 --> 00:01:21.466
That install is two slash commands, one after the other. The first points Claude Code at Cloudflare's official plugin marketplace.

10
00:01:21.466 --> 00:01:33.233
The second installs the plugin, which does two jobs at once: it adds the Cloudflare Skills, and it registers the API, docs, bindings, builds and observability MCP servers.

11
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:42.250
Then you authorise. The first time it calls a Cloudflare tool, your browser opens and you sign in and approve access.

12
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:54.122
It is a standard OAuth handshake: there is no API key to find, copy or store. You choose which permissions to grant, and which account, if you belong to more than one.

13
00:01:54.122 --> 00:02:01.657
To confirm the servers are connected, run /mcp inside Claude Code, or claude mcp list from a normal shell.

14
00:02:02.357 --> 00:02:10.787
For your first instruction, read something before you change something. Ask it to list the zones on your account and their plans.

15
00:02:10.787 --> 00:02:17.909
Then something small and safe: create a KV namespace called scratch-test, and show me how to delete it again.

16
00:02:17.909 --> 00:02:23.333
Or from a Workers folder, the classic: deploy this Worker and give me the live URL.

17
00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:30.128
It tells you what it is about to do before it touches anything, and that review step is your safety net.

18
00:02:30.828 --> 00:02:40.371
Now the gotcha, and it is worth knowing before you hit it. The MCP servers cover the API, but building and shipping Workers is Wrangler's job.

19
00:02:40.371 --> 00:02:51.458
And a browser wrangler login session does not include every permission scope: some account-level actions, and Turnstile among them, need scopes OAuth will not grant.

20
00:02:51.458 --> 00:02:59.993
When you hit that wall, create a scoped API token and expose it as an environment variable. Wrangler picks it up automatically.

21
00:03:00.693 --> 00:03:11.150
For continuous integration, a headless server, or simply tighter control over what the agent can touch, a scoped API token is the right tool.

22
00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:19.457
Grant only the permissions the job needs, and scope it to a specific account or zone rather than to all of them.

23
00:03:19.457 --> 00:03:30.879
You can also restrict it by client IP and set a time to live, which is sensible for automation. And copy the secret immediately: Cloudflare shows it once.

24
00:03:31.579 --> 00:03:36.763
There is one more distinction worth knowing. A user token is tied to you personally.

25
00:03:36.763 --> 00:03:47.626
An account token is tied to the account, and it keeps working even if a person leaves the team, which makes it the better choice for shared services and continuous integration.

26
00:03:47.626 --> 00:03:50.898
Both are accepted by Wrangler and by the MCP servers.

27
00:03:51.598 --> 00:04:00.109
And remember what you are doing here: you are handing an agent the keys to live infrastructure. A few habits keep that safe.

28
00:04:00.109 --> 00:04:11.092
Least privilege, so a token that only edits DNS cannot deploy Workers. One token per purpose or machine, so you can revoke one without breaking everything else.

29
00:04:11.092 --> 00:04:19.740
Never commit a token. OAuth for you, tokens for machines. And roll it on any doubt, because the secret only shows once anyway.

